  "answer_text": "**AltaVista** was one of the most iconic and groundbreaking search engines of the early internet era. \n\nIf you type `altavista.com` into your browser today, you will be redirected to Yahoo Search. However, in the mid-to-late 1990s, AltaVista was the undisputed king of search and the gateway to the World Wide Web for millions of early internet users.\n\nHere is a quick look at why AltaVista was so revolutionary, and what happened to it:\n\n### 1. The Tech Behind It (1995)\nLaunched on **December 15, 1995**, AltaVista was created by researchers Paul Flaherty, Louis Monier, and Michael Burrows at **Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC)**. It was originally designed as a showcase for DEC\u2019s incredibly fast, 64-bit \"Alpha\" supercomputer servers. \n\nBefore AltaVista, searching the web was slow and heavily reliant on manual directories (like the original Yahoo! Directory). AltaVista changed the game by being:\n*   **The first \"full-text\" index:** It didn't just search titles or meta tags; it actually indexed the entire text of web pages.\n*   **Incredibly fast:** It used a highly efficient web crawler named **Scooter** that could index millions of pages a day, returning search results in milliseconds\u2014something unheard of at the time.\n*   **Advanced search capabilities:** It was the first major search engine to support complex boolean operators (`AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, and exact phrase searching using quotation marks).\n\n### 2. Pioneer of Web Translation (Babel Fish)\nIn 1997, AltaVista launched **Babel Fish**, the world\u2019s first free, web-based machine translation service. Before Google Translate, millions of internet users used \"Babelfish\" to instantly translate text and entire websites between languages.\n\n### 3. The Portal Trap and the Rise of Google\nDespite its massive technical superiority and popularity, AltaVista fell victim to the late-90s \"portal craze.\" \n\n*   In 1998, DEC was acquired by Compaq.\n*   Compaq tried to redesign AltaVista, transforming it from a clean, lightning-fast search box into a cluttered \"web portal\" packed with news, weather, shopping, and email (trying to copy Yahoo!).\n*   This bloat frustrated users who just wanted to search. \n*   Around this time, a minimalist, hyper-focused search engine named **Google** emerged in 1998. Google's clean homepage and superior PageRank algorithm quickly began stealing AltaVista\u2019s user base.\n\n### 4. The End of an Era\nBy the early 2000s, AltaVista had lost the search wars. After a series of acquisitions, it was bought by **Overture Services**, which was subsequently bought by **Yahoo!** in 2003. Yahoo! eventually retired the AltaVista search technology and ran its own search engine under the AltaVista name.\n\nOn **July 8, 2013**, Yahoo! officially shut down AltaVista for good. Since then, navigating to `altavista.com` simply redirects to Yahoo! Search, marking the end of one of the internet's most important technical pioneers.",
